window.docContents[63].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Classical-Wave-Equations--Why-Characteristics-Organize-Hyperbolic-Theory--Duhamel___s-Principle___-Forcing-with-Zero-Cauchy-Data":{"contents":"A source acting at time  contributes a homogeneous wave with zero initial\ndisplacement and initial velocity . At time  that contribution\nhas evolved for . Duhamel's principle integrates these delayed responses\nover , using the homogeneous formulas.\n\nLet , put , and let\n be  within the closed forward\nhalfspace . Then there exists\n satisfyingFor every , both initial traces hold jointly in space and\ntime:The  field is defined on all signed times. The forcing regularity\nassumption is only within the forward halfspace; no support or growth\ncondition is imposed.\n\nIn dimension one, integrate the retarded d'Alembert formula. In higher\ndimensions, write  or ; in either case  and use\nthe corresponding mean formula. For nonnegative time, the witness has the formwhere  is the corresponding homogeneous wave\nwith displacement zero and velocity . Clipping the forcing\nindex to  extends the construction to signed times. The retarded\nintegral regularity and equation theorems give joint  regularity and\nthe forced residual. The collapsed integral and its first derivative give\nzero Cauchy data; continuity of the field and its time derivative gives\nthe two joint traces.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Classical Wave Equations\u0009Why Characteristics Organize Hyperbolic Theory","header":"7.2.3.1.2. Duhamel's Principle: Forcing with Zero Cauchy Data","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Classical-Wave-Equations--Why-Characteristics-Organize-Hyperbolic-Theory--Duhamel___s-Principle___-Forcing-with-Zero-Cauchy-Data"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Weak-solutions":{"contents":"Momentum test fields are smooth and compactly supported and vanish at the\nterminal time, but they may be nonzero at .  Integration by parts then\ngivesThe integrability predicate requires spatial integrability of the combined\nmomentum integrand at every physical time, and time integrability of its\nspatial integral rather than of each summand. The initial pairing is\nintegrable by the  initial datum and compactly supported smooth test.\nThis pressure-retaining identity is used\nby a weak solution with a pressure representative; solenoidal tests remove its pressure term.\n\nA pressure-free weak solution tests momentum only against solenoidal fields. It stores a velocity with  slices at every time in\n, an explicit distributional spatial gradient  with \nslices and weak-derivative identities at every time in , the combined\niterated integrability of the momentum integrand, weak incompressibility of\nevery velocity slice, and the initial datum as a boundary functional. The\ninitial datum is required only to lie in , and forcing has \nspatial slices at every physical time. The viscous term uses , never a\nclassical derivative of the weak velocity.A weak solution with a pressure representative additionally supplies an  pressure slice at\nevery physical time and the pressure-retaining identity for all momentum\ntests. Restricting to solenoidal tests gives its checked projection to\na pressure-free weak solution; the converse would require pressure reconstruction.\n\nThe componentwise gradient witnesses use the\nweak-derivative relation developed in the\nSobolev chapter. The fluid formulation adds the momentum identity,\nincompressibility, pressure, and time dependence; it keeps the same weak\ncalculus.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Stokes and Incompressible Navier–Stokes","header":"8.5.1.5. Weak solutions","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Weak-solutions"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Whole-space-formulas":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel is treated in every dimension. Its convolution with bounded\ncontinuous data solves the heat equation at positive time and attains the datum\nlocally uniformly, and Duhamel's formula solves the forced problem for globally\n, compactly supported sources. The weak maximum and comparison principles\nhold for classical solutions on compact parabolic cylinders. The strong maximum\nprinciple and the Harnack inequality of Evans §7.1.4 hold for uniformly\nelliptic nondivergence operators with continuous coefficients ( and\n). For measure data, the porous-medium\nBarenblatt profile has prescribed\npositive mass and a Dirac initial trace.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Classical and Weak Heat Flow","header":"6.8.1.1. Whole-space formulas","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Whole-space-formulas"}});